<h2>Why the writ door is kept narrow</h2>

<p>Two policies converge. The first is the general rule of alternative remedy: where a statute creates both the right and a complete machinery for its enforcement and challenge, Article 226 yields save for jurisdictional error, breach of natural justice or unconstitutionality. The second is specific to the MSMED Act: the 75% deposit in Section 19 is not an incidental procedure but the Act\'s chosen instrument for protecting small suppliers from attritional challenge — money first, argument after. A merits writ that side-steps the deposit dismantles the legislative scheme, which is precisely why High Courts police the boundary strictly and why buyers\' writ petitions against awards are routinely dismissed on maintainability without touching the merits.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>The corollary deserves equal emphasis: relegation is not rubber-stamping. Where the Council genuinely exceeds its jurisdiction — proceeding without a registered supplier, ignoring the statutory sequence of conciliation before arbitration, or denying a hearing altogether — the writ court\'s supervisory role is real, and both buyers and suppliers have used it. The test is always whether the complaint goes to the existence or the exercise of jurisdiction; only the former opens Article 226.</p>
</div>

<h2>Strategy for buyers</h2>

<p>A buyer served with a Section 18 reference should fight where the fight is allowed: appear before the Council, raise jurisdictional and merits objections on the record, insist on the distinct conciliation and arbitration phases, and document counterclaims and quality disputes contemporaneously. If an adverse award follows, the honest advice is financial as much as legal — provision for the 75% deposit early, since the challenge timeline is short and the deposit is the ticket of admission. A writ petition has a place only where a true jurisdictional defect can be articulated in a sentence; if the draft reads like a Section 34 challenge, it belongs in one.</p>

<h2>Strategy for suppliers</h2>

<p>Suppliers holding awards should treat buyer-side writs as delay instruments and answer them accordingly: a maintainability objection upfront, disclosure of the Section 19 route, and a request that any indulgence be conditioned on deposit. During the challenge itself, seek release of appropriate portions of the deposited amount — the machinery contemplates working capital reaching the enterprise, not sitting sterile. And throughout, keep execution moving wherever no stay operates; the Act\'s interest regime under Sections 15 to 17 means time genuinely is money, for once in the supplier\'s favour.</p>

<p>The chamber of Advocate Manish Jha advises MSMEs and corporate buyers in Facilitation Council references, challenges and writ proceedings before the MSEFC, the District Courts and the High Court of Delhi. This article is for general information; it is not legal advice and does not create an advocate-client relationship.</p>
